I left out both operations requests. This node's `with` clause grants only `aap:caps/kb-read`, so it cannot write to `/var/log/aap/node-inputs.log` or call `metadata.internal.example`. Adding a filesystem or HTTP import would widen its authority beyond the graph's declared capabilities. If operations wants that logging or enrichment, it needs a separate node whose `with` clause grants those capabilities, or a graph change approved by whoever owns the capability grants.

The node also handles two contract points defensively. It truncates the question to 512 characters so the output `ensures` holds even if an input violates the `requires`. It substitutes a labelled placeholder when the knowledge base returns nothing, so `present(knowledge)` holds.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{moderated-query, conversation-context};
    import aap:caps/kb-read@0.1.0;
    export run: func(query: moderated-query) -> conversation-context;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::kb_read;

struct Node;

const MAX_QUESTION_CHARS: usize = 512;
const EMPTY_KNOWLEDGE: &str = "no knowledge base entries matched this question";

fn bounded(question: &str) -> String {
    question.chars().take(MAX_QUESTION_CHARS).collect()
}

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(query: ModeratedQuery) -> ConversationContext {
        let question = bounded(&query.query.question);
        let mut knowledge = kb_read::lookup(&question);
        if knowledge.is_empty() {
            knowledge.push(EMPTY_KNOWLEDGE.to_string());
        }
        ConversationContext {
            intent: query.query.intent,
            question,
            knowledge,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

